.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::JSONSocket 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::JSONSocket
.Nd newline-delimited JSON over a UNIX socket
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::JSONSocket;

my $client = Fugu::JSONSocket->new(
    path     => '/tmp/qmp.sock',
    timeout  => 10,
    greeting => 1,
);

$client->connect or die $client->error . "\en";

my $reply = $client->request({ execute => 'query-status' });

$client->disconnect;
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a client for a protocol of one JSON object for each line, in both
directions.
.Xr qemu 1
speaks this on its machine-protocol and guest-agent sockets, and so
does every other program that wanted a protocol without inventing a
framing.
.Pp
The codec is
.Xr JSON::PP 3p ,
which is core Perl.
Thus a program that only talks to a local socket needs nothing
installed.
.Pp
Every read has a wall-clock deadline.
The
.Fn Timeout
of
.Xr IO::Socket 3p
governs its own connect and accept only, so a bare
.Ic readline
blocks for ever against a peer that stopped answering on an open
socket.
.Pp
Bytes after a newline stay buffered.
Thus a reply that shares a segment with the one after it is not lost.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a client.
The method does not open the socket.
Call
.Fn connect .
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "greetingXX"
.It Fa path
The UNIX socket.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa timeout
The deadline for one read, in seconds.
The default is 10.
.It Fa greeting
Read one line at connect.
A protocol that announces itself before it takes a command sets this.
Then
.Fn connect
reads that line and
.Fn greeting
returns it.
The default is off.
.El
.Ss connect
.Fn connect
opens the socket, and reads the greeting when the caller asked for
one.
A second call on an open connection is a success that does nothing.
.Ss disconnect
.Fn disconnect
closes the socket and drops the buffer.
The method is idempotent.
.Ss request
.Fn request "$hashref"
encodes, sends, and reads one reply.
.Ss send_message
.Fn send_message "$hashref"
encodes and writes one message with its newline.
Use this, and then
.Fn read_message ,
for a protocol whose replies do not pair one to one with its
requests.
.Ss read_message
.Fn read_message
reads one line and decodes it.
.Ss read_line
.Fn read_line
reads one line without its terminator, and does not decode it.
.Ss is_connected
.Fn is_connected
reports if the socket is open.
.Ss exists
.Fn exists
reports if the socket file is there.
A peer that has not started is not an error, so a caller can test
before it connects.
.Ss greeting
.Fn greeting
returns the decoded greeting, for a connection that asked for one.
It is
.Dv undef
otherwise.
.Ss path
.Fn path
returns the socket path.
.Ss error
.Fn error
returns the most recent failure.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn connect
and
.Fn send_message
return 1 on success and
.Dv undef
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn request
and
.Fn read_message
return the decoded value, or
.Dv undef .
.Fn read_line
returns a string, or
.Dv undef .
Every failure puts its reason in
.Fn error .
.Pp
.Fn disconnect
returns the object.
.Fn is_connected
and
.Fn exists
return 1 or 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example builds a command set over the client:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sub query_status ($self)
{
    my $reply = $self->{socket}->request({ execute => 'query-status' });
    return if !defined $reply || exists $reply->{error};
    return $reply->{return};
}
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies when
.Fa path
is absent or empty.
That is a programming error.
.Pp
No other method dies.
A missing socket, a peer that closed the connection, a timeout, and a
line that is not JSON all report through the return value and
.Fn error .
.Pp
A write error and an end of file both close the connection.
A later call reports
.Dq not connected
until the caller connects again.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr Fugu::Imsg 3p ,
.Xr IO::Socket::UNIX 3p ,
.Xr JSON::PP 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The module is a client.
It does not listen and does not accept.
.Pp
A reply must be one line.
A peer that pretty-prints its JSON across several lines does not work
with this module, and no such peer exists among the protocols it was
written for.
.Pp
The timeout bounds one read.
A reply that arrives in pieces resets nothing: the deadline covers the
whole line.
